# Deploy

One folder of `.work` files. We never rewrote it for a phone, a CDN, or a server.
Two ideas, kept separate:

- **Targets** — *where the nexus lives*: `local` or `cloud`. One image, two homes.
- **Delivery modes** — *how a page reaches a browser*: three of them, no rewrite
  (see [[Delivery modes]]).

## The weave is the build

`work bundle` walks the tree, resolves every reference into a code-graph edge,
audits that every `grant:` is fulfillable, compiles each foreign unit to wasm, and
runs the tests. The output is one self-contained gzipped `salesapp.html`. A red test
ships **no bundle** — the build is also the audit.

server compute :weave do
  def build(tree) do
    with {:ok, graph} <- resolve_refs(tree),
         {:ok, _caps} <- audit_caps(graph),
         {:ok, units} <- compile_units(graph),
         {:ok, :green} <- run_tests(units) do
      {:ok, emit_html(units, graph)}
    end
  end
end

## Two targets, one image

A target is *where the nexus lives*, and both run the **same OCI image** — local is
that image in a container on your machine, cloud is the same image on a Machine that
scales to zero. Not a fork: one runtime, two homes.

server compute :targets do
  defmodule Target do
    defstruct where: :local, image: "ghcr.io/workbooks-sh/runtime:latest", scale_to_zero: false
  end

  def local, do: %Target{where: :local}
  def cloud, do: %Target{where: :cloud, scale_to_zero: true}
end

`work deploy` reads this and places the image at the chosen home. Next: how the page
actually reaches a reader — [[Delivery modes]].
